{"updateTextStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":145028,"endIndex":145029,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"textStyle":{"fontSize":{"magnitude":12,"unit":"PT"}},"fields":"bold,italic,underline,backgroundColor,fontSize"}},
{"insertText":{"location":{"index":144719,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"text":"\nCross-Dataset Transfer Performance"}},
{"updateParagraphStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":144720,"endIndex":144755,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"paragraphStyle":{"namedStyleType":"NORMAL_TEXT","alignment":"JUSTIFIED","lineSpacing":200,"indentFirstLine":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"spaceAbove":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"spaceBelow":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"keepWithNext":true},"fields":"namedStyleType,alignment,lineSpacing,indentFirstLine,spaceAbove,spaceBelow,keepWithNext"}},
{"updateTextStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":144720,"endIndex":144754,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"textStyle":{"fontSize":{"magnitude":12,"unit":"PT"},"bold":true},"fields":"bold,italic,underline,backgroundColor,fontSize"}},
{"updateTextStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":144754,"endIndex":144755,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"textStyle":{"fontSize":{"magnitude":12,"unit":"PT"},"bold":true},"fields":"bold,italic,underline,backgroundColor,fontSize"}},
{"insertText":{"location":{"index":144719,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"text":"\nNo ROC-AUC difference between SwinFusion++ and either encoder was detectable (all Holm-adjusted p = 1.000; raw p from 0.198 to 0.925), and every paired 95% interval lay between −0.011 and +0.009 AUC, which is incompatible with a large difference in either direction. SwinFusion++ had the lower point estimate in six of the eight comparisons. The ceiling left little room to detect a gain: in seven of the eight comparisons, even the largest gain still possible was smaller than the difference the test could detect with 80% power (Appendix J, Table J6), so these results do not establish equivalence. In the supplementary paired intervals for threshold-dependent metrics (Table J2), SwinFusion++ had higher accuracy than Swin-B on Celeb-DF v2 (+2.35 percentage points [+0.08, +5.10]; approximate, nine fake-class clusters) and than Video Swin-B on DeeperForensics-1.0 (+1.74 percentage points [+0.50, +3.23]). Because the corresponding ROC-AUC differences were not detectable, these gains are best read as differences in where the frozen thresholds fell rather than as better separation of real and fake videos. Within each benchmark, SwinFusion++ was therefore statistically indistinguishable from both encoders in ROC-AUC: combining the two encoders did not lower in-distribution ROC-AUC by a detectable amount, every paired interval excludes a loss larger than 0.011, and all three models operated near the ceiling of the metric."}},
